{"sdk_http_response": {"headers": {"Content-Type": "application/json; charset=UTF-8", "Date": "Mon, 21 Sep 2026 09:01:41 GMT", "Server": "scaffolding,on,HTTPServer2", "Content-Encoding": "gzip", "Vary": "Origin,X-Origin,Referer", "x-gemini-service-tier": "standard", "x-xss-protection": "0", "x-frame-options": "SAMEORIGIN", "x-content-type-options": "nosniff", "server-timing": "gfet4t7; dur=25224", "Via": "1.1 google", "Alt-Svc": "h3=":443"; ma=2592000", "Transfer-Encoding": "chunked"}, "body": null}, "candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"media_resolution": null, "code_execution_result": null, "executable_code": null, "file_data": null, "function_call": null, "function_response": null, "inline_data": null, "text": "**Final Thoughts on Minangkabau Sentence Creation**

Okay, so the task is clear: craft one fluid, natural sentence in Minangkabau, drawing vocabulary mainly from the "Word Candidates" list, aiming for about 15 words. No jargon, just everyday language. And it has to be original; no copying from the examples provided. Right, let's see... the candidate list seems pretty good. "Kaciak-kaciak," "batigo," "basamo," those jump out as useful starting points. Ah, "mambari" (to give), "mananami" (to plant), those could be fun to weave in, too. Now, let's brainstorm a scenario. A family situation feels natural. Maybe siblings working together? That feels right... I could picture siblings, maybe in a garden, or on a farm. Okay, let's start sketching. "Kami batigo badunsanak paduli jo padusi tu nan sadang mambicaroan soal tanah pusako di kampuang." (We three siblings care about that woman who is currently discussing the ancestral land issue in the village). That *sounds* pretty good, but it has some words that aren't on the list, so let's start again, simpler and more in tune with the vocabulary. Okay, let's focus on plants or a farm. That sounds good, and more natural than ancestral land! I want to try this: "Kami basamo-samo batani untuak mamanuhi kaparaluan kaluargo salamo hiduik di parantauan." (We farm together to meet the family's needs while living in the migration area). Okay, not bad, that's only eleven words. I need more. I want to use more of the word candidates. "Ambo batigo jo adiak-adiak sadang mandiruih tinam-tinaman kaciak-kaciak di muko rumah amak." (I, along with my younger siblings (three of us), am watering small plants in front of mother's house). Ah, 'muko rumah' is not that natural, not as nice as other possible options. "Ambo batigo badunsanak" (We three siblings), now that *sounds* natural! Right. Let's build from that. Okay, "Ambo batigo badunsanak sadang mandiruih tinam-tinaman kaciak-kaciak di halaman." That's watering small plants in the yard. That's ten words. I have to expand. Let's see... Maybe specify what kind of plants? "Ambo batigo badunsanak saibu sadang mandiruih tinam-tinaman kaciak-kaciak di sampiang rumah amak." (We three siblings of the same mother are watering small plants beside mother's house). Okay, it is getting good. That's 14 words. Now, I have to make the final push. Ah, it is 15 words! That sounds perfect. All candidates accounted for. So, is it correct? Yes! It is correct! The final sentence: "Ambo batigo badunsanak saibu sadang mandiruih tinam-tinaman kaciak-kaciak nan ditanam amak di sampiang rumah kami." (The three of us, siblings from the same mother, are watering the small plants that mother planted beside our house). It's done.


